<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>See traffic on your phone</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Connect your navigation app over Bluetooth to see traffic and review its requests. Confirm changes on the Go&rsquo;s screen.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="by-name">The Go appears by name, and <span class="highlight">three connections</span> receive its traffic at once.</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">skyBlip Go+ 00012A</span>: the product, then the
        24-bit address it got when it was <%= link_to "activated", briefing_activation_path %>.
        No pairing or code is needed. The Go sends traffic as a FLARM NMEA data stream. It supports both Bluetooth serial connection types that navigation software looks for, so the app you already fly with can find it.
      </p>
      <p>The Go also sends its GPS position over the same Bluetooth link once a second. Before its GPS fix, it sends the UTC time alone as soon as its receiver knows it, marked as having no position. Your navigation app can use it on an iPad without GPS when you fly with the Go as its source. <%= link_to_page "blog/gps-to-tablet", label: "How the Go supplies position and traffic to your tablet" %>.</p>
      <p>
        The tablet on the yoke, the phone in a pocket and the passenger&rsquo;s
        iPad all see the same traffic. Only one app at a time can change
        settings: the first app to save settings keeps that right until it
        disconnects. The second app is told which app holds it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot shot--photo">
      <%= content_image_tag "pages/index/skyblip-go-techo.avif", alt: "A skyBlip Go in a white case with its antenna and the traffic radar on its e-paper screen" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Connect without pairing, a code or an account</li>
    <li>Use three connections at once</li>
    <li>Connect through Nordic UART or FLARM-style profiles</li>
    <li>Use SDVFR Next, SkyDemon, ForeFlight and the rest</li>
    <li>The device keeps your latest flights</li>
    <li>Download them over the same link on the ground</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="asked">A press allows, <span class="highlight">a tap refuses</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    An app can request an update to the device&rsquo;s software, called firmware, a restart into the USB installation program, called the bootloader, a settings change, deletion of your flights or a shutdown. The device shows the request on its own screen and waits thirty seconds. Press the button labelled <span class="mono">ACCEPT</span> to allow it. Tap the pad labelled <span class="mono">CANCEL</span> to refuse it. The message <span class="mono">CANCELS IN 30 S</span> counts down the time remaining. If you do not answer within that time, the request is refused.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= screen_image_tag "confirm.png", alt: "The authorization prompt for settings sent by the phone: SETTINGS under the large mark, the change it makes, CANCELS IN with the seconds left, the button's key ACCEPT and the pad's key CANCEL" %>
    <figcaption>The screen asks before saving settings</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="lede">
    Your callsign is one of those settings. The app sends it. The screen asks
    for permission. Once approved, it is shown on the status screen and broadcast
    once every ten seconds. It can appear on nearby aircraft&rsquo;s screens if their devices can read that message. You can also type it on the
    device itself, in <%= link_to_page "controls", label: "the callsign field", anchor: "callsign" %>,
    or set it from a browser in <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %>, beside
    the aircraft type, the units and the alarm.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    A press only counts once the question is on screen and you have stopped
    pressing: a series of presses used to change a setting cannot also
    authorise a request.
  </p>

  <h3 id="firmware">Install an update <span class="highlight">from a browser</span>.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %> sends a signed
    firmware file over the same Bluetooth link, from Chrome or Edge. The screen asks for permission
    once and names the version before the device accepts the file and installs it. The new
    firmware must then start its radio, receive data from its receiver and update its
    screen before it is kept. If it cannot, the device returns to the
    previous firmware and the settings you had when the update began.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    If the device cannot read your settings, it tells you. It uses
    its defaults. The self test&rsquo;s <span class="mono">STORAGE</span> row
    reads <span class="mono">NVS+NOR DEFAULTS</span>. The phone is told:
    check the callsign and the aircraft type before you fly.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    If the firmware will not install at all, open recovery mode from the same page. Press the device&rsquo;s button to confirm. The device switches itself off with the screen shown below. Press the button again to start the USB bootloader. Connect the device with a USB cable. It appears as a drive named <span class="mono">TECHOBOOT</span>. Copy a <span class="mono">.uf2</span> file onto that drive to install new firmware. Press <span class="mono">RESET</span> to return to skyBlip. Nothing transmits during recovery. Two presses of <span class="mono">RESET</span> open the same drive without a phone.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <%= screen_image_tag "recovery.png", alt: "The recovery page: RECOVERY under the large mark, the USB drive name TECHOBOOT, COPY A .UF2 FILE TO IT, TO GO BACK, PUSH RESET, and the button's key START" %>
    <figcaption>The device is ready for USB recovery</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3 id="no-pairing">Why there is no pairing.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Bluetooth pairing encrypts the data fields known as GATT characteristics. This
    causes a known Web Bluetooth failure on Windows and would break
    <%= link_to "the browser page that installs firmware", briefing_root_path %>.
    Security therefore relies on checking the firmware file, not on securing the link.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>The bootloader checks the firmware signature against our key</li>
    <li>It refuses firmware older than the installed version</li>
    <li>A Bluetooth connection without pairing is not enough to authorise a firmware installation</li>
    <li>All other requests can only run on the ground</li>
    <li>The device refuses them unless you confirm on its screen</li>
    <li>Your presence at the device replaces a pairing code</li>
  </ul>
</section>

